    "body": "Proposed by the maintainer (filed by an agent), 2026-11-09: \"a option to align on three points is probably more sane, if we gonna have several ways of aligning a model to a workplane.\"\n\n**What:** a setup alignment mode that picks three points on the model (vertices or points on faces). The three points define a plane, which is laid onto the chosen G17/G18/G19 plane like \"face to plane\" (#330): the plane's normal is rotated onto the work plane's normal, the model is translated so the plane lies in it, and the part stays on the correct side. Optionally, point 1 becomes the origin and point 1 to 2 sets the X direction (a full 3-point frame, the common shop \"3-2-1\" setup).\n\n**Why:** it works on parts with no single clean face (castings, freeform, scanned meshes), and it avoids the face/edge snapping problem.\n\n**Requirements:**\n- It goes through one undoable command, validated at creation, and refuses by name: points that are collinear or too close (a degenerate plane), non-finite input, or no model.\n- It uses the same apply path as face-to-plane, so the two can't disagree.\n- Setup page UI: pick points 1, 2 and 3 with a clear prompt and a clear/redo.\n\n**Done means:** tests for three points on a tilted face laying it flat on G17 (normal -Z, z = 0, part above); collinear points refused by name; undo restores; and, if the frame variant is done, the origin and X direction set from points 1 and 2.\n\nRelated: #330 (face to plane, reopened), and the face-only pick issue filed alongside this one.\n",
